{"title":"Fjord Chemicals","sub":"A record year, a record price: is it a trap?","type":"Private equity","industry":"Chemicals","geo":"Norway","diff":3,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Private equity · cyclical earnings · leverage risk","prompt":"Fjord makes specialty coatings for ships and offshore platforms. It just had its best year ever: $80M of EBITDA. The owners want 8× that, $640M, and banks will lend 5× this year's EBITDA. A PE fund asks you whether to bid, needing 2.5× its money in five years.","clarify":[{"q":"History?","a":"Demand follows shipbuilding and offshore investment, which move in cycles of roughly 5–7 years."},{"q":"Outlook?","a":"Order books for new ships are falling from their peak."},{"q":"Exit?","a":"Similar businesses sell for about 7× EBITDA mid-cycle."},{"q":"Debt paydown?","a":"About $100M over five years in a normal cycle."}],"structure":{"root":"Should the fund bid $640M for Fjord?","branches":[{"head":"Earnings","items":["Peak vs average through the cycle"]},{"head":"Returns","items":["Entry on peak, exit mid-cycle"]},{"head":"Debt risk","items":["Leverage at a downturn"]},{"head":"Price","items":["What to offer"]}]},"hypothesis":"The price is set on a peak year. If earnings return to their average, the fund pays far too much, and debt set on peak earnings could become dangerous in a downturn.","questions":[{"q":"Look at Fjord's EBITDA history. What do you notice?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"394.471438pt\" height=\"177.853pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 394.471438 177.853\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 177.853 L 394.471438 177.853 L 394.471438 0 L 0 0 L 0 177.853 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 41.311438 152.745 L 387.271438 152.745 L 387.271438 14.145 L 